<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Shilluk Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1490 CE – 1861 CE (approx.) · capital Fashoda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the Shilluk people on the White Nile, famous for its sacred kingship centred on the reth and the founder-hero Nyikang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shilluk-people">Shilluk</a> tradition, the kingdom was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyikang">Nyikang</a>, who led his people north along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> and settled them on its west bank. Historians date the establishment of the kingship to around the late 15th century, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fashoda">Fashoda</a> becoming the royal centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The reth was a sacred king, believed to embody the spirit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyikang">Nyikang</a>; his health was linked to the wellbeing of the land, a case made famous by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-frazer">James Frazer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-e-evans-pritchard">E. E. Evans-Pritchard</a>. Settlements were dense along the river, living from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and fishing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shilluk-people">Shilluk</a> canoe fleets raided as far north as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 19th century slave traders and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turco-egyptian-sudan">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</a> broke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shilluk-people">Shilluk</a> power; Egyptian forces subdued the kingdom around 1861. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fashoda">Fashoda</a> later gave its name to the Anglo-French crisis of 1898. The reth survives today as a traditional ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shilluk-kingdom.jpg" alt="Shilluk Kingdom" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Shilluk.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1490 CE – 1861 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fashoda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shilluk traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shilluk</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-sudan">South Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chollo, Shilluk reth</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1676&amp;focus=shilluk-kingdom" aria-label="Shilluk Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5566 1809 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shilluk Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5886 2065L5888 1853"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5888 1853h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5886 2065h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5886 2065h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1676&amp;focus=shilluk-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

